From f7aa4174dbc860065051551aee450dabf56b3191 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ahmed Syed <Ahmed.Syed@windriver.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:43:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Fix MariaDB rejoin failure after unclean shutdown

After an unclean stop of a node hosting a mariadb-server replica (power
loss, IPMI power-off, kernel panic) the replica does not rejoin the
Galera cluster when the node returns. The pod stays 0/1 and is restarted
by the startupProbe until the application is removed and reapplied.

The trigger is the transaction coordinator log. MariaDB creates
/var/lib/mysql/tc.log while the server runs and removes it on a clean
shutdown, so finding it at startup means the previous stop was unclean
and crash recovery still has to replay it.

run_mysqld() performs local housekeeping before starting the
replicating server: it sets the admin account passwords and runs
mariadb-upgrade against a local mariadbd started with --wsrep-on=false
so those statements are not replicated. That is where recovery fails,
because the wsrep engine registered in tc.log is not enabled in this
invocation:

  [ERROR] Recovery failed! You must enable all engines that were
          enabled at the moment of the crash
  [ERROR] Can't init tc log
  [ERROR] Aborting

Replay the log before that launch instead, using the same wsrep-enabled
recovery pass the script already runs to obtain a wsrep position. With
the provider loaded the engine set matches what tc.log recorded, so the
replay succeeds, resolves the in-doubt transactions and removes the
log, leaving nothing for the --wsrep-on=false start to choke on.

Recovering rather than deleting matters. It discards no XA state, so it
is correct on every path, including a single-node bootstrap or an
all-nodes-down election winner where this node's data may be the only
copy. That is why the handling is not conditional on the cluster
argument.

The replay is followed by a check that tc.log is actually gone rather
than by trusting the recovered position, which is scraped from
mariadbd's log text. A pass that failed but still printed a position
line would otherwise send the wsrep-disabled start into the same abort
on every restart with nothing ever clearing the log.

recover_wsrep_position() is promoted from a nested closure to a module
level function so both callers can use it, and it now returns None
instead of raising when no position is printed. The caller that records
a seqno keeps the old strictness by raising on None, since a seqno
cannot be written without one. It also bounds the pass with a timeout so
an overrun is reported rather than appearing as an anonymous probe
restart, and logs a non-zero exit.

A second defect kept the pod from reporting a failure.
wait_mysql_status() looped without a limit and kept no handle on the
thread running mariadbd, so once the local server aborted the script
waited on a process that had already gone. Bound the wait, pass the
thread so an exited mariadbd is detected within one cycle, and raise
when the local server never becomes available. The thread is marked
daemon so that raise can actually terminate the interpreter; left
non-daemon, shutdown blocks joining a thread still waiting on an
unusable mariadbd and the container stays Running and never Ready,
which is the state the bound exists to avoid.

Startup budget: the replay runs first and the status wait follows it, so
the two are consecutive and their sum has to stay inside the
startupProbe deadline rather than sitting alongside it. With the
defaults that worst case is 300 + 10 x 30 = 600s against a 660s
deadline. All three values are exposed under conf.galera as
mysql_recovery_timeout, mysql_status_max_retries and
mysql_status_retry_delay and passed through the statefulset, following
the existing cluster_leader_ttl pattern, so they can be stretched
alongside the probe parameters. They are read with os.getenv(...) or
default rather than a default argument, because the statefulset always
sets them and the case to defend against is an empty value rather than
an absent one.

Change-Id: I5fcbda32393b925d1d20ce6e7cfd64be2f677e05
Signed-off-by: Ahmed Syed <ahmed.syed@windriver.com>
---
